{"title":"Career Roadmap · Signalling Engineer","role":"Signalling Engineer","summary":"A Signalling Engineer designs, tests, integrates, maintains, and improves railway signalling and train-control systems that ensure trains operate safely, efficiently, and with adequate separation.","facts":{"Current degree":"B.Tech / B.E. (Bachelor of Technology / Engineering)","Course":"Railway Engineering / Rail Transportation"},"about":[],"intro":[],"horizons":[],"sections":[{"heading":"Career Overview","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A Signalling Engineer designs, tests, integrates, maintains, and improves railway signalling and train-control systems that ensure trains operate safely, efficiently, and with adequate separation."},{"type":"p","text":"The role sits at the intersection of:"},{"type":"flow","items":["Railway Operations","Electronics","Control Systems","Communication","Safety Engineering","Software","Automation"],"sep":"+"},{"type":"p","text":"Modern signalling increasingly combines conventional interlocking with electronic interlocking, automatic train protection, centralized traffic control, digital communication, onboard systems, and data-driven maintenance."},{"type":"h","text":"Core Railway Signalling Flow"},{"type":"flow","items":["Train / Track Occupancy","Trackside Detection","Interlocking","Route Verification","Signal / Movement Authority","Train Control System","Train Movement","Continuous Monitoring"]}]},{"heading":"What Does a Signalling Engineer Do?","kind":"other","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Typical responsibilities include:"},{"type":"list","items":[{"text":"Railway signalling-system design"},{"text":"Route and interlocking design"},{"text":"Signal placement and engineering"},{"text":"Track-circuit and axle-counter systems"},{"text":"Point-machine systems"},{"text":"Level-crossing protection"},{"text":"Electronic interlocking"},{"text":"Automatic train protection"},{"text":"Train detection"},{"text":"Train control"},{"text":"Railway co